---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../../components/PageHeader.astro';
import Tldr from '../../components/Tldr.astro';
import TryItNow from '../../components/TryItNow.astro';
import CopyPrompt from '../../components/CopyPrompt.astro';
import KeyTakeaways from '../../components/KeyTakeaways.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import { href } from '../../lib/site';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="M365 Plugins"
  description="Use Claude inside Excel, Word, PowerPoint, and Outlook — building, editing, and drafting right inside your files without breaking your formulas or formatting."
  pageId="m365"
  readMinutes={6}
>
  <PageHeader
    icon="📎"
    title="Claude in Microsoft 365"
    subtitle="You don't always have to go to Claude. Sometimes Claude comes to you — into a sidebar inside the Office apps your day already runs on."
    readMinutes={6}
  />

  <Tldr>
    <p>
      Claude add-ins live <strong>inside Excel, Word, PowerPoint, and Outlook</strong> — in a sidebar,
      working directly in your file. The theme throughout: Claude isn't generating a <em>new</em> file
      off to the side and handing it back. It's working <em>inside your file</em>, respecting the
      formulas, styles, and structure that are already there.
    </p>
  </Tldr>

  <div class="prose-content">
    <p>
      There's a version of Claude that lives on a tab in your browser. Then there's the version that
      shows up inside the app you already have open. The M365 add-ins are the latter — a Claude sidebar
      embedded directly into the Microsoft tools your day runs on. Here's what that actually unlocks,
      app by app.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- Excel -->
  <section class="mb-10 reveal">
    <h2 class="mb-1 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">📈 Excel — the one finance should care about most</h2>
    <p class="mb-4 text-muted">This is the deepest of the integrations. Claude reads your whole workbook — not just the cell you're on, but the formulas, the dependencies, and how the tabs connect.</p>

    <div class="grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2 mb-5">
      <div class="hover-lift rounded-[12px] border border-border bg-card p-4">
        <p class="mb-1 font-semibold text-text">Build from scratch or a template</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">A DCF, a budget-vs-actual, a cash-flow projection with scenario toggles — created from your assumptions or dropped into a template you already use.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="hover-lift rounded-[12px] border border-border bg-card p-4">
        <p class="mb-1 font-semibold text-text">Modify without breaking things</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">It changes values and inputs while <em>preserving your formulas and dependencies</em>, so the model still works after — and it can sort, filter, edit pivot tables, and apply conditional formatting.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="hover-lift rounded-[12px] border border-border bg-card p-4">
        <p class="mb-1 font-semibold text-text">Debug formulas</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">Point it at a <code>#REF!</code> mess or a circular reference and ask what broke — it traces the source and proposes a fix that keeps the sheet intact.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="hover-lift rounded-[12px] border border-border bg-card p-4">
        <p class="mb-1 font-semibold text-text">Full transparency</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">Every change is highlighted and explained, with citations you can click straight to the referenced cells. Nothing happens off in the dark.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <CopyPrompt text="Build a 12-month cash-runway model from the assumptions on the Inputs tab, with a row for each capital draw. Preserve the existing formulas, and highlight every cell you change so I can review it." />
  </section>

  <!-- Word -->
  <section class="mb-10 reveal">
    <h2 class="mb-1 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">📄 Word — long documents, done right</h2>
    <p class="mb-4 text-muted">Word is where Claude earns its keep on the heavy stuff: policies, procedures, proposals, spec documents.</p>

    <div class="grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-3 mb-5">
      <div class="hover-lift rounded-[12px] border border-border bg-card p-4">
        <p class="mb-1 font-semibold text-text">Native tracked changes</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">Claude's edits arrive as real tracked changes — you review and accept or reject them exactly like edits from a colleague, not as a mystery rewrite.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="hover-lift rounded-[12px] border border-border bg-card p-4">
        <p class="mb-1 font-semibold text-text">Template-aware drafting</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">Hand it your template and it drafts <em>into</em> your format, inheriting your heading and paragraph styles instead of pasting in something generic.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="hover-lift rounded-[12px] border border-border bg-card p-4">
        <p class="mb-1 font-semibold text-text">Read a redline back</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">When a counterparty returns a document full of tracked changes, Claude can summarize what they changed and group it by severity — so you know what to look at first.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <CopyPrompt text="Review this SOP against our template, fix the formatting to match, and leave your changes as tracked edits so I can approve them one by one." />
  </section>

  <!-- PowerPoint -->
  <section class="mb-10 reveal">
    <h2 class="mb-1 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">📊 PowerPoint — outline to deck</h2>
    <p class="mb-4 text-muted">Because nobody ever said "I wish I spent more time making slides."</p>

    <div class="grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-3 mb-5">
      <div class="hover-lift rounded-[12px] border border-border bg-card p-4">
        <p class="mb-1 font-semibold text-text">Reads your template</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">Slide master, layouts, fonts, colors — Claude uses them when it builds slides, so you get <em>your</em> brand instead of an off-brand generic deck.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="hover-lift rounded-[12px] border border-border bg-card p-4">
        <p class="mb-1 font-semibold text-text">Outline → deck</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">Give it the structure or the source content and it drafts the slides — a skeleton in seconds, not hours.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="hover-lift rounded-[12px] border border-border bg-card p-4">
        <p class="mb-1 font-semibold text-text">Reformat existing decks</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">Clean up spacing, restyle to the template, or condense a bloated deck down to what actually needs to be said.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <CopyPrompt text="Turn this one-page project summary into a six-slide deck using our template, with speaker notes. Follow the slide master's fonts and colors — don't introduce any new styling." />
  </section>

  <!-- Outlook -->
  <section class="mb-10 reveal">
    <h2 class="mb-1 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">📧 Outlook — your chief of staff</h2>
    <p class="mb-4 text-muted">Rolling out across the suite: in Outlook, Claude helps you triage the inbox, arrange meetings, and draft replies in your voice.</p>

    <div class="grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-3 mb-5">
      <div class="hover-lift rounded-[12px] border border-border bg-card p-4">
        <p class="mb-1 font-semibold text-text">Draft replies in your voice</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">Give Claude the context ("push back on this deadline but stay friendly") and it writes the reply. You edit, you send.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="hover-lift rounded-[12px] border border-border bg-card p-4">
        <p class="mb-1 font-semibold text-text">Summarize long threads</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">That 47-message chain from last Tuesday? Ask "what do I actually need to do?" and get the action items without reading the whole saga.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="hover-lift rounded-[12px] border border-border bg-card p-4">
        <p class="mb-1 font-semibold text-text">Turn chaos into a follow-up</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">Rambling thread, no clear owner. Claude synthesizes it into a clean follow-up email ready to send to the whole group.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <CopyPrompt text="Summarize this email thread and tell me: (1) what decisions were made, (2) what I need to do, and (3) anything still unresolved." />
  </section>

  <!-- Shared context -->
  <section class="mb-10 reveal">
    <h2 class="mb-1 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">🔗 The real magic: shared context</h2>
    <p class="mb-4 text-muted">Here's the part that ties it together.</p>
    <div class="prose-content">
      <p>
        Once the add-ins are installed, <strong>context travels across your Office apps in one
        conversation</strong>. Start a model in Excel, move to PowerPoint to build the deck, pull the
        numbers into a Word memo — Claude already knows what everything means, no re-explaining. As
        Anthropic puts it: change one thing, and every open file catches up. For anyone who's ever
        manually redone a slide every time a spreadsheet cell changed, that's the whole ballgame.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Callout variant="cowork" title="Which apps share context">
      <p>
        Shared context spans <strong>Word, Excel, and PowerPoint</strong> — all generally available on
        our plan. <strong>Outlook</strong> is in beta and joining the same conversation, so treat it as
        the newest, still-settling member of the family.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </section>

  <Callout variant="code" title="⚠️ Watch for prompt injection here too">
    <p>
      A spreadsheet or template from an outside party can hide malicious instructions in cells or
      comments — the same risk you'll read about on the
      <a href={href('/chrome/')} class="text-gold underline underline-offset-2">Claude in Chrome</a> page.
      Only point Claude at files you trust, and never at a random workbook downloaded from the internet.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <Callout variant="gold" title="✅ One reassurance worth repeating">
    <p>
      Because we're on <strong>Claude Enterprise</strong>, your conversations and files aren't used to
      train the model. When you point Claude at a financial model or a contract, that work stays yours.
      More on the <a href={href('/safety/')} class="text-gold underline underline-offset-2">Safety &amp; Data</a> page.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <TryItNow title="Try it in Excel">
    <p>
      Open a workbook you know well — ideally one with a few tabs that feed each other. Open the Claude
      sidebar and ask it to explain how the file actually works:
    </p>
    <CopyPrompt text="Walk me through how this workbook calculates its final number, tab by tab. Cite the specific cells and formulas you're referencing so I can follow along." />
    <p>
      You'll get a guided tour of your own model — the fastest way to feel how deep the Excel
      integration really goes.
    </p>
  </TryItNow>

  <Callout variant="gold" title="📣 Availability & rollout">
    <p>
      The Claude Office add-ins are generally available on our plan and rolling out across Aptera now.
      To install, open Excel, Word, or PowerPoint → <strong>Insert</strong> tab → <strong>Get Add-ins</strong>
      → search <em>"Claude by Anthropic."</em> Don't see it, or want a hand getting set up? Email
      <strong><a href="mailto:jake@aptera.us" class="text-gold underline underline-offset-2">jake@aptera.us</a></strong>.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <KeyTakeaways>
    <ul>
      <li>Claude add-ins work <strong>inside</strong> Excel, Word, PowerPoint, and Outlook — editing your file, not handing back a new one.</li>
      <li><strong>Excel</strong> is the deepest: it modifies models while preserving formulas and dependencies, and explains every change with clickable cell citations.</li>
      <li><strong>Word</strong> edits arrive as native tracked changes; <strong>PowerPoint</strong> follows your slide master and brand.</li>
      <li><strong>Shared context</strong> lets one conversation flow across Word, Excel, and PowerPoint without re-explaining a thing — change one input and every open file catches up (Outlook is in beta).</li>
      <li>Chain this with <a href={href('/chrome/')} class="text-gold underline underline-offset-2">Claude in Chrome</a> — gather in the browser, produce the deliverable in Office, no copy-paste seam.</li>
    </ul>
  </KeyTakeaways>
</BaseLayout>
